%description:

RFC 5494 -- a packet with an opcode reserved for experiments provokes nothing.

Checks RFC826-RECV-10 from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc826/catalog.md and
RFC5494-NUM-3 from doc/project/evidence/standard/rfc5494/catalog.md. The English procedure
is in
doc/project/evidence/protocol/arp/checks/input-validation.md#an-experimental-opcode; the
program steps below map onto its expected observations. Mapping of the shared link mockup:
host A = hostA, host B = hostB, host C = hostC, switch S = switch.

Every field of the crafted packet is that of a valid request from host A to host B, except
the opcode, which is 24 -- OP_EXP1 of RFC 5494 section 3. The target protocol address is the
address of host B on purpose, so that the packet reaches the opcode question, which is the
last question of the reception algorithm of RFC 826.

Defect. This test FAILS and the failure is NOT declared expected, because the model claims the
behaviour: Arp::processArpPacket switches on the opcode and has a `default:` branch for one it does
not know, so the unknown case is handled. What that branch does is throw
(Arp.cc:358). A branch that exists and does the wrong thing is a defect, and a station that stops is
the strongest possible way to fail the rule.

What the run shows. The simulation model stops the run on an opcode it does not know:
Arp::processArpPacket switches on the opcode and its default branch throws
"Unsupported opcode %d in received ARP packet"
(src/inet/networklayer/arp/ipv4/Arp.cc). RFC 826 answers the question in one sentence --
"Negative conditionals indicate an end of processing and a discarding of the packet" -- and
a station that stops is the strongest possible way to fail it. Expected observation 3, that
host B goes on working, is the observation that catches this. The run ends with an error and
never prints a verdict line, so the assertion on the verdict line does not find it.
See doc/project/evidence/model/arp/results.md.

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"
#include "ArpMutations.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

// OP_EXP1, the first opcode that RFC 5494 section 3 reserves for experiments.
static const int OP_EXP1 = 24;

static Packet *buildExperimentalOpcodePacket(const CaptureStore&)
{
    return buildArpPacket("craftedOpcode", "hostB.eth[0]", OP_EXP1,
            interfaceMacAddress("hostA.eth[0]"), interfaceIpv4Address("hostA.eth[0]"),
            interfaceMacAddress("hostB.eth[0]"), interfaceIpv4Address("hostB.eth[0]"));
}

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("rfc5494_experimental_opcode")
        // Expected observation 1: the crafted packet arrives at host B, opcode 24.
        .inject(inject("hostB").into("eth[0]", "upperLayerOut").at(0.5)
                  .describe("an ARP packet with the experimental opcode 24, addressed to host B")
                  .packet(buildExperimentalOpcodePacket))
        // Expected observations 2 and 3: host B puts no ARP packet on the link afterwards,
        // and host B goes on working to the end of the run (RFC826-RECV-10, RECV-1).
        .never(on("hostB")
                  .match([](const MatchContext& c) { return arpPacketLeaves(c.event, "hostB"); })
                  .describe("any ARP packet from host B")
                  .within(0.4));
}

%activity:
// present only so opp_test compiles the %global above; this module is not in the network.
EV << "rfc5494_experimental_opcode compiled\n";

%file: test.ned

import inet.node.ethernet.Eth100M;
import inet.node.ethernet.EthernetSwitch;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;

// The shared link mockup: three hosts on one Ethernet segment, joined by a switch.
network ArpSharedNet
{
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator { @display("p=100,50"); }
        switch: EthernetSwitch { @display("p=400,150"); }
        hostA: StandardHost { @display("p=250,100"); }
        hostB: StandardHost { @display("p=250,250"); }
        hostC: StandardHost { @display("p=550,150"); }
    connections:
        hostA.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostB.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
        hostC.ethg++ <--> Eth100M <--> switch.ethg++;
}

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
network = ArpSharedNet
sim-time-limit = 1s
seed-set = 0

# No host sends a datagram: the crafted packet is the only stimulus of the run.

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST rfc5494_experimental_opcode: PASS
